ence`\n- bundle_present: `True`\n- verification_report: `work/results/t06/claude/20261028_204856/paper/scientific-evidence/verification-report.json`\n- total_references: `50`\n- resolved_references: `8`\n- anchored_references: `42`\n- dangling_references: `0`\n- manifests_checked: `2`\n\n## Agentic evidence audit\n- checked: `True`\n- verdict: `fail`\n- finding_count: `2`\n- summary: The numeric results, qualification statements and selection and unscored-attempt disclosures match the evidence trail. Two implementation-specific claims about the evaluator overstate what the readable source in source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py shows.\n\n### Audit findings to fix\n- `lbfgs-crosscheck-overclaim` (Method (Scoring and verification) and Supplementary Information A (Evaluator Specification)): The evaluator source runs the L-BFGS reference fit once, in crosscheck_fit(). That function takes only the smallest case (by K, then n) and only its first min(n,600) rows. verify_edit() uses fit_bt alone, with no L-BFGS comparison on any edited refit or on each unedited case. Neither the per-budget-level cross-check nor the per-case cross-check is supported.\n  - claim: For each budget level, the evaluator refits the edited data with its own fitter, cross-checked against an independent L-BFGS fit. Appendix A: with an independent L-BFGS cross-check on each unedited case.\n  - evidence: source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py: reference_fit (line 375) is called only from crosscheck_fit (lines 580-590). crosscheck_fit is invoked once (line 985). verify_edit (around line 405) calls only fit_bt. paper.tex Section 3.3 and Appendix A.\n  - requested_fix: Replace both statements with: the evaluator's Newton fitter was cross-checked once against an independent L-BFGS fit on a slice of at most 600 rows of the smallest case. Remove the wording that implies a cross-check on every edited refit or every unedited case.\n- `influence-baseline-same-computation` (Introduction (What this paper is and is not), Method (Reference policies) and Results (Reference policies set the scale)): The evidence supports only that the two family-averaged scores are equal (0.6056607653 each) and that the margin is therefore 0.0. It does not support that the computations are identical. The candidate discards rows with first-order effect >= 0 and non-finite effects. baseline_influence in the evaluator does not discard rows with effect >= 0, and it uses the evaluator's damped-Newton fit. Row-level identity of the edit sets is not shown.\n  - claim: the evaluator's own influence baseline is numerically identical to the studied search; the evaluator computes its own first-order influence baseline with the same formulas; the margin is zero because the evaluator's influence baseline is the same computation as the studied search\n  - evidence: source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py _propose (the filter effect[i] < 0). source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py baseline_influence (lines 456-468, only an np.isfinite filter). paper_data.json final_evaluation.metrics: score_influence = combined_score = 0.6056607653243968 and margin_vs_best_baseline = 0.0.\n  - requested_fix: Say that the evaluator's influence baseline uses the same first-order formulas, and that its family-averaged score equals the studied search's (0.606), which gives a margin of exactly zero. Drop the claims that the two are the same computation or numerically identical as procedures, or note that the baseline omits the non-negative-effect filter.\n\n## Soft warnings\n- figures[0] labels too long without abbreviation\n- figures[0].font_sizes.title below minimum: 8.5 < 10.0\n- figures[0].font_sizes.axis below minimum: 8.0 < 9.0\n- figures[0] PNG width too small: 1339 < 2400\n- figures[1].font_sizes.title below minimum: 9.5 < 10.0\n- figures[1] PNG width too small: 1686 < 2400\n# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. Three targeted retrieval needs, none requiring a rewrite of the paper story.\n\n## Overall assessment\nThe draft is densely cited on Bradley-Terry estimation (ties, order effects, existence of the MLE, penalised fits, asymptotics) and on sampling uncertainty. It also cites the single-point influence-function paper, the dropping-data robustness paper, the two Arena integrity papers, and the Arena and MT-Bench data sources. All quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are out of scope here. The gaps concern the paper's actual subject (small adversarial edits to comparison data), the caveats on first-order approximations, and the sports data.\n\n## Targets\n\n### 1. Poisoning and manipulation literature (high, positioning) — Related Work\n- **Anchor:** the \"Influence and robustness to dropping data\" and \"Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards\" paragraphs.\n- **Why:** the paper asks how few dropped or reversed rows displace rank 1, which is a data-poisoning or manipulation question. No citation covers influence-based poisoning, label-flip attacks, or adversarial manipulation of rank aggregation and rating systems. The \"generic, evaluator-verified probe\" framing cannot be positioned without this, and reviewers would likely flag it.\n- **Search topics:** influence-function-based data poisoning; label-flipping attacks on logistic or pairwise models; adversarial robustness of rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons; manipulation-resistant rating systems (Elo / BT).\n\n### 2. Accuracy of first-order group-removal approximations (medium, background) — Problem Setting and Method\n- **Anchor:** \"(i) Additivity. The per-row effects are summed without modelling how p_i and A change…\". The \"Linearisation error\" hypothesis in the analysis section relies on the same claim.\n- **Why:** the claim that the additive first-order heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2% budget is uncited. Only single-point influence and the dropping-data robustness paper are cited.\n- **Search topics:** accuracy of influence functions for group effects; infinitesimal-jackknife approximation error when dropping data in M-estimators or logistic regression.\n\n### 3. Sports rating context (medium, benchmark_context) — Problem Setting and Method\n- **Anchor:** Table 1 row \"Sports: NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first\".\n- **Why:** NBA results are used as a fourth BT design (and called a \"sports proxy\" in Limitations) without any citation to BT or Elo-style rating of sports teams. One or two references would justify the design and the home-team-first and tie conventions. The Arena and MT-Bench rows are already cited.\n- **Search topics:** Bradley-Terry rating of sports teams with home advantage; Elo rating applied to basketball or team sports.\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- BT model variants and estimation: already over-supported.\n- Uncertainty of rankings: adequately cited.\n- LLM-judge and Arena data descriptions: covered by the Chiang and Zheng citations.\n- Numerical results, reference-policy scores and validity checks: grounded in `paper_data.json`.\npostdraft_citation_weave_agent_claude_20261028_212829.log\npostdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261028_212829.log\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\npostdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\npostdraft_reference_retrieval.log\npostdraft_references.bib\npostdraft_references_provenance.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67422bf1-0c6e-48c7-8ffa-5e0ce2de1e91","uuid":"6f7d3db3-0845-47c4-a04b-b928a2b69d5d","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:52:35.560Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"VENUE.md\nclaims_grounding.json\nconcept_figure_run.log\ndraft_reference_gaps.json\ndraft_reference_gaps.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_agent_claude_20261028_212829.log\ndraft_reference_gaps_prompt.md\ndraft_reference_gaps_validation_feedback.json\ndraft_referen