{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVNpHZNFU7kuwp4NVDPz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01J4cCznZuNTWJW3mswpE2mE","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; grep -n -i -E \"L-BFGS|LBFGS|identical|same computation|same first-order|same formula|crosscheck|cross-check\" paper.tex claims_grounding.json | cut -c1-600; wc -l paper.tex; cat paper_evidence_audit_feedback.md | head -5; tail -5 memory.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4815,"cache_read_input_tokens":36643,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4815,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67422bf1-0c6e-48c7-8ffa-5e0ce2de1e91","uuid":"534d1e30-f246-4700-8dda-5dc97c82cb01","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:52:37.648Z","request_id":"req_011CgVNpH1NaiozqAXmhyt8Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J4cCznZuNTWJW3mswpE2mE","type":"tool_result","content":"claims_grounding.json:85:   \"claim\": \"Three evaluation seeds yield identical scores (0.6057); no confidence interval available; diagnostics unverified.\",\npaper.tex:70:The studied search is the standard first-order influence heuristic, not a new algorithm, and we do not claim an improvement over any baseline: the evaluator's own influence baseline is numerically identical to the studied search, so the margin over the best reference policy is exactly zero (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\npaper.tex:130:For each budget level, the evaluator refits the edited data with its own fitter, cross-checked against an independent L-BFGS fit, and ignores any quantity the proposer reports about gaps or ranks. The score of a level is $1$ if the original top competitor is no longer first, and otherwise $\\mathrm{clip}(1-G_{\\text{after}}/G_{\\text{before}},0,1)$, the fraction of the gap that was closed. The \\emph{edit success score} of a case is the mean over the five budgets and over evaluation seeds; the score of a design is the mean over its cases; and the \\emph{family-averaged} edit success i\npaper.tex:133:Three evaluator-owned reference policies, which never contact the proposer, calibrate the scale of the scores. The \\emph{constant} policy averages the score of an empty edit and of editing the first $k$ rows in file order. The \\emph{random} policy averages 20 uniformly random edit sets of the same size. The \\emph{win-rate heuristic} selects rows in which the incumbent top competitor won, preferring rows against the runner-up. The evaluator also computes its own first-order influence baseline with the same formulas as Section~\\ref{sec:influence}. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/\npaper.tex:158:The proposer is evaluated once on the sealed final data with three evaluation seeds (201, 202, 203). A seed only resets proposer state; it never selects data or workload. Because the proposer is deterministic, the three seeds are replicate executions of the same computation and carry no information about sampling variability of the data. Search-time scores were obtained on a separate proxy data collection (different Arena partition and NBA seasons, and no expert-human or LLM-judge data) and are reported only as separate context in Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}; they are never comp\npaper.tex:204:This is one evaluation value per policy without an uncertainty estimate and without per-design values for the reference policies; we therefore make no claim about which of the two policies is better. The margin of the studied search over the best reference policy is $0.0$, because the evaluator's influence baseline is the same computation as the studied search (both score $0.606$). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.margin_vs_best_baseline]]\npaper.tex:209:The three evaluation seeds produce identical family-averaged scores ($0.6057$ each), as expected for a deterministic proposer on fixed data. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\npaper.tex:242:\\paragraph{No sampling uncertainty and no noise-versus-intent attribution.} The identical seeds reflect determinism, and no resampling was performed; we therefore cannot say whether the observed vulnerability exceeds ordinary sampling variation of the leaderboard, nor whether it corresponds to plausible noise or to deliberate manipulation. We also do not model what an adversary could actually do on a real platform.\npaper.tex:264:Design scores average cases within a design; family weights are $0.25$ each. The evaluator computes ranks and gaps from its own Newton fit, with an independent L-BFGS cross-check on each unedited case, and the target is judged on the original rank-1 competitor's gap to the best other competitor on the refit of the edited data.\npaper.tex:271:\\caption{All retained metrics of the sealed final evaluation (three evaluation seeds, identical across seeds). Displacement rates are at the 2\\% budget.}\npaper.tex:296:Three seed-level records were produced (seeds 201, 202, 203), each with the identical family-averaged score $0.6057$ and recorded durations of $23.1$, $11.8$ and $11.6$ seconds. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\npaper.tex:297:The identical scores are expected: the proposer is deterministic, a seed never selects data or workload, and the anonymisation of competitor indices is fixed for each case by the evaluator. Durations vary by a factor of two between records and we do not attribute the difference to any cause. The evaluator labels candidate-produced diagnostics as unverified; none of our statements relies on them, and all quantities are computed by the trusted refit.\npaper.tex:308:The frozen configuration is identical to the starter code of the task (same source tree hash), so the studied method and the evaluator's influence baseline share this logic. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#_propose]]\n321 paper.tex\n# Agentic evidence audit feedback\n\n- generated_at: `2026-10-28T21:52:31.847687+00:00`\n- ok: `False`\n- verdict: `fail`\n\n## Repair pass (audit + figure manifest)\n- Removed ungrounded design size / \"six models\" claims (Table 1, competitors bullet); reworded permuted-control claim to the single control run per case; limited Yang2026Preference/Seetharaman2021Influence descriptions to title-supported content; softened Giordano/Holy/Szczecinski descriptions.\n- Concept-figure render failed (rc=1): shipped documented matplotlib fallback (scripts/build_overview.py, PDF+PNG), manifest uses list-valued categories/series_per_panel and generator=matplotlib, attempted_generator=generate-concept-figure.\n- validate_claims passes (14 claims, 30 refs); manifest validates against schema; paper compiles (13 pages incl. refs/SI).","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"s