{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVNpSyAKeZv4M5QahiPt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Sv4V95GouVnskoZ7kkQpCu","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; sed -n 70p paper.tex; sed -n 130p paper.tex; sed -n 133p paper.tex; sed -n 200,206p paper.tex; sed -n 262,266p paper.tex; sed -n 305,310p paper.tex; grep -n -i \"numerically\\|same computation\" claims_grounding.json paper.tex | cut -c1-200"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2276,"cache_read_input_tokens":41458,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2276,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67422bf1-0c6e-48c7-8ffa-5e0ce2de1e91","uuid":"c6dd3cf8-c66f-48d8-bb99-1ba90c8e31b1","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:52:39.646Z","request_id":"req_011CgVNpSGz5b5MekK5r6866"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Sv4V95GouVnskoZ7kkQpCu","type":"tool_result","content":"The studied search is the standard first-order influence heuristic, not a new algorithm, and we do not claim an improvement over any baseline: the evaluator's own influence baseline is numerically identical to the studied search, so the margin over the best reference policy is exactly zero (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\nFor each budget level, the evaluator refits the edited data with its own fitter, cross-checked against an independent L-BFGS fit, and ignores any quantity the proposer reports about gaps or ranks. The score of a level is $1$ if the original top competitor is no longer first, and otherwise $\\mathrm{clip}(1-G_{\\text{after}}/G_{\\text{before}},0,1)$, the fraction of the gap that was closed. The \\emph{edit success score} of a case is the mean over the five budgets and over evaluation seeds; the score of a design is the mean over its cases; and the \\emph{family-averaged} edit success is the equally weighted ($0.25$ each) mean over the four designs. The \\emph{top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget} of a design and mode is the mean of the displacement indicator at the largest budget over the cases and seeds in that cell. Invalid or missing answers count as worst case. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\nThree evaluator-owned reference policies, which never contact the proposer, calibrate the scale of the scores. The \\emph{constant} policy averages the score of an empty edit and of editing the first $k$ rows in file order. The \\emph{random} policy averages 20 uniformly random edit sets of the same size. The \\emph{win-rate heuristic} selects rows in which the incumbent top competitor won, preferring rows against the runner-up. The evaluator also computes its own first-order influence baseline with the same formulas as Section~\\ref{sec:influence}. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#baseline_scores]]\n\n\\subsection{Reference policies set the scale}\nPanel (b) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} compares the studied search with the evaluator's reference policies on the same family-averaged scale. The constant and random policies score $0.071$ each, so, averaged over all cases, a generic perturbation of at most 2\\% of the rows closes only a small fraction of the gap. This suggests that the displacement obtained in crowd and sports data owes more to targeted row selection than to perturbation in general, although per-design values for the random policy are not available to check it. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_constant]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_random]]\nThe win-rate heuristic, which needs no model refit, scores $0.574$ against $0.606$ for the influence search, a difference of $0.032$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_winrate]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_influence]]\nThis is one evaluation value per policy without an uncertainty estimate and without per-design values for the reference policies; we therefore make no claim about which of the two policies is better. The margin of the studied search over the best reference policy is $0.0$, because the evaluator's influence baseline is the same computation as the studied search (both score $0.606$). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.margin_vs_best_baseline]]\nThe task protocol declares a separate claim of outperforming the reference policies, which requires a positive margin and a paired-bootstrap interval excluding zero; that claim is not met here. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/task_contract.json#protocol.promotion_rule]]\n\nThe evaluation interface is a request--response protocol with a reset operation (clearing all proposer state and seeding it) and a propose operation. Each propose request carries the mode, the budget $k$, the number of competitors, the number of rows and base64-encoded integer arrays of competitor indices and outcomes, with rows in the order of the original data. Each call has a hard wall-clock limit of 30 seconds, and the proposer runs on CPU with two threads. Responses with extra keys, non-integer or duplicate or out-of-range indices, more than $k$ indices, or tie rows in flip mode are invalid and score zero for that level. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#handle]]\nThe sealed final evaluation has twelve cases (crowd, expert human and LLM judge: one data source $\\times$ two modes; sports: three seasons $\\times$ two modes), three primary seeds and one permuted control episode per case, with each episode issuing five budget calls. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#CASE_SPECS]]\nDesign scores average cases within a design; family weights are $0.25$ each. The evaluator computes ranks and gaps from its own Newton fit, with an independent L-BFGS cross-check on each unedited case, and the target is judged on the original rank-1 competitor's gap to the best other competitor on the refit of the edited data.\n\n\\subsection{Complete Final Metric Table}\n\\item For every row compute $p_i$, $\\tilde y_i$ and $s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}$, and the effect $\\Delta_i$ of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} for the requested mode; set $\\Delta_i=+\\infty$ for tie rows in flip mode.\n\\item Sort rows by $(\\Delta_i,\\,i)$, keep the first $k$, and drop those with $\\Delta_i\\ge0$ or non-finite $\\Delta_i$. Return the indices.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe frozen configuration is identical to the starter code of the task (same source tree hash), so the studied method and the evaluator's influence baseline share this logic. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#_propose]]\nIts seed state is reset on request but is never used to make choices.\n\npaper.tex:70:The studied search is the standard first-order influence heuristic, not a new algorithm, and we do not claim an improvement over any baseline: the evaluator's own influence baseline is nu\npaper.tex:158:The proposer is evaluated once on the sealed final data with three evaluation seeds (201, 202, 203). A seed only resets proposer state; it never selects data or workload. Because the pro\npaper.tex:204:This is one evaluation value per policy without an uncertainty estimate and without per-design values for the reference policies; we therefore make no claim about which of the two polici","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67422bf1-0c6e-48c7-8ffa-5e0ce2de1e91","uuid":"357c2d88-d088-4c59-8bda-2ef34053dbf1","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:52:39.719Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"The studied search is the standard first-order influence heuristic, not a new algorithm, and we do not claim an improvement over any baseline: the evaluator's own influence baseline is numerically identical to the studied search, so the margin over the best reference policy is exactly zero (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\nFor each budget level, the evaluator refits the edited data with its own fitter, cross-checked against an independent L-BFGS fit, and ignores any quantity the proposer reports about gaps or ranks. The score of a level is $1$ if the original top competitor is no longer first, and otherwise $\\mathrm{clip}(1-G_{\\text{after}}/G_{\\text{before}},0,1)$, the fraction of the gap that was closed. The \\emph{edit success score} of a case is the mean over the five budgets and over evaluation seeds; the score of a design is the mean over its cases; and the \\emph{family-averaged} edit success is the equally weighted ($0.25$ each) mean over the four designs. The \\emph{top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget} of a design and mode is the mean of the displacement indicator at the largest budget over the cases and seeds in that cell. Invalid or missing answers count as worst case. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#aggregate]]\nThree evaluator-owned reference policies, which never contact the proposer, calibrate the scale of the scores. The \\emph{constant} policy averages the score of an empty edit and of editing the first $k$ rows in file order. The \\emph{random} policy averages 20 uniformly random edit sets of the same size. The \\emph{win-rate heuristic} selects rows in which the incumbent top competitor won, preferring rows against the runner-up. The evaluator also computes its own first-order influence baseline with the same formulas as Section~\\ref{sec:influence}. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#baseline_scores]]\n\n\\subsection{Reference policies set the scale}\nPanel (b) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} compares the studied search with the evaluator's reference policies on the same family-averaged scale. The constant and random policies score $0.071$ each, so, averaged over all cases, a generic perturbation of at most 2\\% of the rows closes only a small fraction of the gap. This suggests that the displacement obtained in crowd and sports data owes more to targeted row selection than to perturbation in general, although per-design values for the random policy are not available to check it. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_constant]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_random]]\nThe win-rate heuristic, which needs no model refit, scores $0.574$ against $0.606$ for the influence search, a difference of $0.032$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_winrate]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_influence]]\nThis is one evaluation value per policy without an uncertainty estimate and without per-design values for the reference policies; we therefore make no claim about which of the two policies is better. The margin of the studied search over the best reference policy is $0.0$, because the evaluator's influence baseline is the same computation as the studied search (both score $0.606$). % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.margin_vs_best_baseline]]\nThe task protocol declares a separate claim of outperforming the reference policies, which requires a positive margin and a paired-bootstrap interval excluding zero; that claim is not met here. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/task_contract.json#protocol.promotion_rule]]\n\nThe evaluation interface is a request--response protocol with a reset operation (clearing all proposer state and seeding it) and a propose operation. Each propose request carries the mode, the budget $k$, the number of competitors, the number of rows and base64-encoded integer arrays of competitor indices and outcomes, with rows in the order of the original data. Each call has a hard wall-clock limit of 30 seconds, and the proposer runs on CPU with two threads. Responses with extra keys, non-integer or duplicate or out-of-range indices, more than $k$ indices, or tie rows in flip mode are invalid and score zero for that level. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#handle]]\nThe sealed final evaluation has twelve cases (crowd, expert human and LLM judge: one data source $\\times$ two modes; sports: three seasons $\\times$ two modes), three primary seeds and one permuted control episode per case, with each episode issuing five budget calls. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/evaluate_final.py#CASE_SPECS]]\nDesign scores average cases within a design; family weights are $0.25$ each. The evaluator computes ranks and gaps from its own Newton fit, with an independent L-BFGS cross-check on each unedited case, and the target is judged on the original rank-1 competitor's gap to the best other competitor on the refit of the edited data.\n\n\\subsection{Complete Final Metric Table}\n\\item For every row compute $p_i$, $\\tilde y_i$ and $s_i=v_{a_i}-v_{b_i}$, and the effect $\\Delta_i$ of Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} for the requested mode; set $\\Delta_i=+\\infty$ for tie rows in flip mode.\n\\item Sort rows by $(\\Delta_i,\\,i)$, keep the first $k$, and drop those with $\\Delta_i\\ge0$ or non-finite $\\Delta_i$. Return the indices.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe frozen configuration is identical to the starter code of the task (same source tree hash), so the studied method and the evaluator's influence baseline share this logic. % @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#_propose]]\nIts seed state is reset on request but is never used to make choices.\n\npaper.tex:70:The studied search is the standard first-order influence heuristic, not a new algorithm, and we do not claim an improvement over any baseline: the evaluator's own influence baseline is nu\npaper.tex:158:The proposer is evaluated once on the sealed final data with three evaluation seeds (201, 202, 203). A seed only resets proposer state; it never selects data or workload. Because the pro\npaper.tex:204:This is one evaluation value per policy without an uncertainty estimate and without per-design values for the reference policies; we therefore make no claim about which of the two polici","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVNpSyAKeZv4M5QahiPt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Tn9EBVXihqrbUGnzxMajpU","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; grep -n -i -E \"poison|Koh|Broderick|group influence|Basu|jackknife|sports\" paper.tex | cut -c1-300"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2276,"cache_read_input_tokens":41458,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2276,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67422bf1-0c6e-48c7-8ffa-5e0ce2de1e91","uuid":"818e8b02-0392-48b0-9c00-4b9d63b85c38","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:52:40.093Z","request_id":"req_011CgVNpSGz5b5MekK5r6866"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tn9EBVXihqrbUGnzxMajpU","type":"tool_result","content":"31:\\title{How Few Edits Displace the Top of a Bradley--Terry Leaderboard? A Verified First-Order Influence Probe on Crowd, Expert, LLM-Judge and Sports Comparison Data}\n42:At the 2\\% budget the search displaces rank 1 in every crowd and sports case, in no LLM-judge case, and for expert human data only by reversing outcomes.\n58:The edits themselves are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search in the spirit of influence functions \\citep{Koh2017Understanding} and approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.\n76:\\item We report that the probe's success is strongly design dependent: edit success scores of 1.000 (crowd), 0.833 (sports), 0.374 (expert human) and 0.216 (LLM judge), and 2\\%-budget displacement in all crowd and sports cases but in no LLM-judge case (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n82:The BT model \\citep{Bradley1952Rank} assigns each competitor a latent score and models the win probability of a pair as a logistic function of the score difference. Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, covariates \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA}, temp\n88:Influence functions estimate the effect of removing or perturbing a training point without refitting \\citep{Koh2017Understanding}. \\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-o\n90:\\paragraph{Data poisoning and label-flip attacks.}\n91:Choosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research; influence-based reasoning has also been used on the defensive side, against poisoning in online learning \\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\citet{Ya\n138:Table~\\ref{tab:designs} lists the designs. Every design is evaluated in both edit modes, so crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge data contribute one case per mode, and sports data three NBA seasons per mode. The sealed final data are disjoint from the data used to choose the search configuration (v\n152:Sports & NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first & 6 (3 seasons $\\times$ 2) & Teams of one season \\\\\n167:Panel (a) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} shows the per-design scores: crowd votes $1.000$, sports $0.833$, expert human $0.374$ and LLM judge $0.216$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_crowd]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.score_sports]] @evidence[[pap\n168:Because a level scores at most one and exactly one only when rank 1 is displaced (up to an exact tie), a design score of $1.000$ means that rank 1 was displaced at every one of the five budgets, including the smallest (0.1\\% of rows), in both crowd cases. The sports score of $0.833<1$ implies th\n179:Rank 1 is displaced in every crowd and sports case in both modes. For expert human data only reversal succeeds, and for the LLM judge neither mode does. Because each crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge cell contains a single case and the proposer is deterministic, every entry is $0$ or $1$; the sp\n180:The ordering by edit success score (crowd, sports, expert human, LLM judge) is thus mirrored by the number of design--mode cells in which the heuristic reaches displacement at 2\\% (2, 2, 1, 0 of 2).\n184:\\caption{Sealed final evaluation: per-design edit success score and top-1 displacement rate at the 2\\% budget by edit mode. Entries in the displacement columns are the means over cases and seeds in the cell (one case for crowd, expert human and LLM judge; three seasons for sports).}\n192:Sports       & 6 & 0.833 & 1.0 & 1.0 \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_sports_drop]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.flip_rate_sports_flip]]\n202:Panel (b) of Figure~\\ref{fig:scores} compares the studied search with the evaluator's reference policies on the same family-averaged scale. The constant and random policies score $0.071$ each, so, averaged over all cases, a generic perturbation of at most 2\\% of the rows closes only a small frac\n215:The measurement supports the statement that, with this heuristic, within these budgets and on these twelve cases, rank-1 vulnerability decreases in the order crowd, sports, expert human, LLM judge. The ordering is an observation for one attack and one target. Failure to displace rank 1 (LLM judg\n232:In the one design where the two modes differ (expert human), reversal succeeds and dropping does not. This is consistent with property (iii) of Section~\\ref{sec:influence}: for the same rows, reversal has a larger first-order effect than removal by the factor $1/|\\tilde y_i-p_i|$. It remains a s\n241:\\paragraph{Design confounds and few cases.} The four designs are specific datasets, not random draws from design classes; they are confounded with size, number of competitors, margin and tie rate. Three of the four designs have one case per edit mode. The NBA data are game results, not preferenc\n247:We measured, with exact-refit verification, how a one-shot first-order influence search fares at displacing the rank-1 competitor of a ridge Bradley--Terry leaderboard using 0.1\\% to 2\\% of the rows, on crowd, expert-human, LLM-judge and sports data. The family-averaged edit success is 0.606, an\n263:The sealed final evaluation has twelve cases (crowd, expert human and LLM judge: one data source $\\times$ two modes; sports: three seasons $\\times$ two modes), three primary seeds and one permuted control episode per case, with each episode issuing five budget calls. % @evidence[[source_evidence\n279:Edit success: crowd / sports & 1.0000 / 0.8328 \\\\\n281:Displacement, drop: crowd / sports / expert human / LLM judge & 1 / 1 / 0 / 0 \\\\\n282:Displacement, flip: crowd / sports / expert human / LLM judge & 1 / 1 / 1 / 0 \\\\","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67422bf1-0c6e-48c7-8ffa-5e0ce2de1e91","uuid":"0b297afe-ded2-4c00-95ea-c8f80a9e59fd","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:52:40.117Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"31:\\title{How Few Edits Displace the Top of a Bradley--Terry Leaderboard? A Verified First-Order Influence Probe on Crowd, Expert, LLM-Judge and Sports Comparison Data}\n42:At the 2\\% budget the search displaces rank 1 in every crowd and sports case, in no LLM-judge case, and for expert human data only by reversing outcomes.\n58:The edits themselves are proposed by a one-shot first-order influence search in the spirit of influence functions \\citep{Koh2017Understanding} and approximate-robustness analysis of ``dropping a little data'' \\citep{Broderick2020Automatic}.\n76:\\item We report that the probe's success is strongly design dependent: edit success scores of 1.000 (crowd), 0.833 (sports), 0.374 (expert human) and 0.216 (LLM judge), and 2\\%-budget displacement in all crowd and sports cases but in no LLM-judge case (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n82:The BT model \\citep{Bradley1952Rank} assigns each competitor a latent score and models the win probability of a pair as a logistic function of the score difference. Extensions handle ties \\citep{Davidson1970OnET}, order effects \\citep{Davidson1977OnET}, covariates \\citep{Yan2025InferenceIA}, temp\n88:Influence functions estimate the effect of removing or perturbing a training point without refitting \\citep{Koh2017Understanding}. \\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-o\n90:\\paragraph{Data poisoning and label-flip attacks.}\n91:Choosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research; influence-based reasoning has also been used on the defensiv