{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVNpd19nPKixpNd5qk9Z","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B3fX4Q6RNC3jb2d5KAWWoU","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; sed -n 88,95p paper.tex | cut -c1-1500; grep -c \"^@\" references.bib; grep -o \"\\\\\\\\cite[tp]*{[^}]*}\" paper.tex | tr ',' '\\n' | sed 's/.*{//;s/}//' | sort -u | wc -l; grep -n \"Additivity\" paper.tex | cut -c1-500; sed -n 1,5p postdraft_reference_query_plan.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4776,"cache_read_input_tokens":43734,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4776,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67422bf1-0c6e-48c7-8ffa-5e0ce2de1e91","uuid":"16cdbf12-cc5c-46fe-8e50-c43295f0bc87","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:52:42.974Z","request_id":"req_011CgVNpcSuwft26REY6krtK"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B3fX4Q6RNC3jb2d5KAWWoU","type":"tool_result","content":"Influence functions estimate the effect of removing or perturbing a training point without refitting \\citep{Koh2017Understanding}. \\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-order influence approximations to find the data to drop. Our search is of this family, specialised to the top-versus-runner-up score gap of a BT fit and extended to outcome reversal. The linear approximation behind such analyses is the infinitesimal jackknife, which \\citet{Giordano2018Swiss} develop as a general approximation to refits with data removed; its accuracy is a property of the problem and of the size of the perturbation, and is not guaranteed for the 2\\% edits studied here. As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\n\n\\paragraph{Data poisoning and label-flip attacks.}\nChoosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research; influence-based reasoning has also been used on the defensive side, against poisoning in online learning \\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\citet{Yang2026Preference} present a preference-poisoning attack on offline reinforcement learning from human feedback. We know these two works only from their titles and describe them no further. Our reversal mode is a label-flip edit and our drop mode a deletion, and the first-order scores in Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} are likewise additive per row. The setting differs: the model is a Bradley--Terry leaderboard rather than a preference-optimised language model, the objective is to dislodge one rank rather than to steer parameters towards a target, and an evaluator refits the exact model for verification. We cite this literature for positioning only and do not compare against any poisoning method, and we have not surveyed attacks on rank aggregation exhaustively.\n\n\\paragraph{Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards.}\n\\citet{Chiang2024Chatbot} describe the Chatbot Arena platform, in which users vote between two anonymous model responses and the votes are aggregated into rankings. \\citet{Min2025Rigging} study strategies by which votes on such platforms can be manipulated to change rankings, and \\citet{Singh2025Illusion} analyse evaluation practices on the same platform, including private testing and differences in data access and sampling across providers. \\citet{Zheng2023Judging} release expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments on MT-Bench. We use the Arena and MT-Bench comparison data, but our attack is a generic, evaluator-verified probe; we do not model any platform's actual voting interface or an adversary's access to it.\n\n30\n30\n125:\\emph{(i) Additivity.} The per-row effects are summed without modelling how $p_i$ and $A$ change after many rows are edited, so the heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2\\% budget even when it is accurate for one row; the accuracy of the underlying linear approximation is problem dependent \\citep{Giordano2018Swiss}, and we did not evaluate it for these data.\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. Five queries, all driven by `draft_reference_gaps.{md,json}`; no retrieval performed in this stage.\n\n## Central topic","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"67422bf1-0c6e-48c7-8ffa-5e0ce2de1e91","uuid":"7c6455c4-4039-4535-98fd-b0e6cdc3e2da","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:52:43.064Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Influence functions estimate the effect of removing or perturbing a training point without refitting \\citep{Koh2017Understanding}. \\citet{Broderick2020Automatic} propose a finite-sample robustness metric asking whether dropping a small fraction of the data can change a conclusion, and use first-order influence approximations to find the data to drop. Our search is of this family, specialised to the top-versus-runner-up score gap of a BT fit and extended to outcome reversal. The linear approximation behind such analyses is the infinitesimal jackknife, which \\citet{Giordano2018Swiss} develop as a general approximation to refits with data removed; its accuracy is a property of the problem and of the size of the perturbation, and is not guaranteed for the 2\\% edits studied here. As in that literature, a first-order search finds candidate edit sets only; we therefore let the evaluator verify every edit by an exact refit.\n\n\\paragraph{Data poisoning and label-flip attacks.}\nChoosing a small set of training points to manipulate so as to change a fitted model is the subject of data-poisoning research; influence-based reasoning has also been used on the defensive side, against poisoning in online learning \\citep{Seetharaman2021Influence}. For preference data, \\citet{Yang2026Preference} present a preference-poisoning attack on offline reinforcement learning from human feedback. We know these two works only from their titles and describe them no further. Our reversal mode is a label-flip edit and our drop mode a deletion, and the first-order scores in Eq.~\\eqref{eq:effects} are likewise additive per row. The setting differs: the model is a Bradley--Terry leaderboard rather than a preference-optimised language model, the objective is to dislodge one rank rather than to steer parameters towards a target, and an evaluator refits the exact model for verification. We cite this literature for positioning only and do not compare against any poisoning method, and we have not surveyed attacks on rank aggregation exhaustively.\n\n\\paragraph{Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards.}\n\\citet{Chiang2024Chatbot} describe the Chatbot Arena platform, in which users vote between two anonymous model responses and the votes are aggregated into rankings. \\citet{Min2025Rigging} study strategies by which votes on such platforms can be manipulated to change rankings, and \\citet{Singh2025Illusion} analyse evaluation practices on the same platform, including private testing and differences in data access and sampling across providers. \\citet{Zheng2023Judging} release expert human and GPT-4 pairwise judgments on MT-Bench. We use the Arena and MT-Bench comparison data, but our attack is a generic, evaluator-verified probe; we do not model any platform's actual voting interface or an adversary's access to it.\n\n30\n30\n125:\\emph{(i) Additivity.} The per-row effects are summed without modelling how $p_i$ and $A$ change after many rows are edited, so the heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2\\% budget even when it is accurate for one row; the accuracy of the underlying linear approximation is problem dependent \\citep{Giordano2018Swiss}, and we did not evaluate it for these data.\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. Five queries, all driven by `draft_reference_gaps.{md,json}`; no retrieval performed in this stage.\n\n## Central topic","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
